      "title": "Helena World Chronicle files antitrust suit against Google and Alphabet",
      "summary": "Arkansas publisher Helena World Chronicle filed a class action lawsuit in the U.S. District Court in D.C. accusing Google and Alphabet of anticompetitive behavior under the Sherman Act, alleging they siphon off publishers' content, readers, and ad revenue, and specifically citing AI technologies like Search Generative Experience (SGE) and Bard AI chatbot as worsening the problem."
    },
    {
      "title": "Helena World Chronicle alleges Google starves free press and loses billions",
      "summary": "Helena World Chronicle, which owns two weekly Arkansas newspapers, argues that Google is starving the free press by sharing publishers' content on Google, causing them to lose billions of dollars."
    },
    {
      "title": "Knowledge Graph launched in May 2012 and grew to 500 billion facts by 2020",
      "summary": "Google's Knowledge Graph, launched in May 2012, displays a 'Knowledge Panel' containing summaries drawn from a database compiled by extracting information from publishers' websites and open source/licensed databases; by 2020, it contained 500 billion facts about 5 billion entities, much of which was allegedly misappropriated from publishers."
    },
    {
      "title": "Featured Snippets and AI technologies shift traffic away from publishers",
      "summary": "Google technologies including 'Featured Snippets' (algorithmically extracting answers from webpages) and AI-based search are cited as shifting traffic away from publishers' websites; a Wall Street Journal report noted that when The Atlantic modeled Google integrating AI into search, 75% of the time AI would answer queries without a click-through, and publishers believe they will lose 20-40% of website traffic when Google's AI products fully roll out."
    },
    {
      "title": "Suit claims Google AI aims to discourage users from visiting publisher websites",
      "summary": "The lawsuit claims Google's AI-based search advances were implemented with the goal of discouraging end-users from visiting publisher websites, arguing that SGE keeps users in Google's 'walled garden' while plagiarizing content and cannot be blocked because it uses the same web crawler (GoogleBot) as general search."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "title": "Helena World Chronicle files antitrust suit against Google and Alphabet",
  "summary": "Arkansas publisher Helena World Chronicle filed a class action lawsuit in the U.S. District Court in D.C. accusing Google and Alphabet of anticompetitive behavior under the Sherman Act, alleging they siphon off publishers' content, readers, and ad revenue, and specifically citing AI technologies like Search Generative Experience (SGE) and Bard AI chatbot as worsening the problem.",
  "tags": ["antitrust", "Google", "Helena World Chronicle", "AI", "class action", "Sherman Act"],
  "body": "Helena World Chronicle, an Arkansas publisher that owns two weekly newspapers, filed a class action lawsuit in the U.S. District Court in D.C. against Google and Alphabet, alleging anticompetitive behavior under the Sherman Act. The suit accuses Google of siphoning off publishers' content, readers, and ad revenue, causing them to lose billions of dollars and starving the free press. The plaintiff specifically cites AI technologies, including Search Generative Experience (SGE) and the Bard AI chatbot, as exacerbating these harms.\n\nThe case alleges that Google's Knowledge Graph, launched in May 2012 and containing 500 billion facts by 2020, misappropriated information from publishers' websites and open source databases. Additionally, the suit references a 2023 News Media Alliance report and a Washington Post article stating that Google's Bard AI was trained on news and magazine publications, with news sites being the third largest category of AI training data.\n\nThe lawsuit seeks damages and an injunction requiring Google to obtain publisher consent to use website data for training AI products, including those of rivals, and allowing opt-out publishers to appear in search results. The case also cites concerns over changing AdSense rates and Google's destruction of chat messages, an issue raised in the Epic Games antitrust lawsuit which Epic won. Hausfeld law firm states that Google's scheme causes profound harm to competition, consumers, labor, and a democratic free press. The plaintiff invokes the Sherman Act and Clayton Act for relief. The U.S. lawsuit follows a Google agreement with the Canadian government where Google pays $73.5 million annually to Canadian news organizations, while negotiations with Meta remain unresolved after Meta blocked Canadian news in August. The case arrives alongside the U.S. Justice Department's lawsuit against Google for monopolizing digital ad technologies, referencing a 2020 DOJ civil antitrust suit over search and search advertising."
}
